+# Hegel property-based testing report for `fixed` 1.31.0
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel] (`hegeltest` 0.28.2 as a
+dev-dependency; the library crate is named `hegel`). All tests live in the
+existing `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` modules of the files whose code they test
+and run with plain `cargo test`. Test functions are named `hegel_*`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### 1. Decimal `Display` -> `FromStr` round trip is exact
+`from_str::tests::hegel_display_from_str_roundtrips_exactly`
+
+For every bit pattern (via `from_bits`) of 19 type aliases spanning signed and
+unsigned, 8/32/64/128 bits, and fractional bit counts 0, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64,
+`x.to_string().parse()` must return exactly `x`. Evidence: the auto-precision
+logic in `display.rs` (`write_frac_dec`) keeps emitting digits until the
+output uniquely identifies the value (the `tie` comparisons), and `FromStr`
+is documented to round to nearest, ties to even. **This property found a real
+bug** (see Suspected bugs #1); the known-buggy inputs are excluded via a
+12-line simulation of the buggy early break (`display_frac_hits_lossy_break`)
+with a comment referencing the canonical pinned failing test.
+
+### 2. Binary/octal/hex `Display` -> `from_str_binary/octal/hex` round trip
+`from_str::tests::hegel_radix_display_from_str_roundtrips_exactly`
+
+Power-of-two radix output is digit-exact (no decimal re-rounding), so
+`{:b}`, `{:o}`, `{:x}`, `{:X}` must all parse back exactly, including
+negative values and full 128-bit patterns. Evidence: existing unit test
+`display::tests::hex` builds expected hex output digit-exactly. Passes for
+all 16 tested type aliases including `U0F128`, `I64F64`, `I128F0`.
+
+### 3. Parsing never panics
+`from_str::tests::hegel_from_str_never_panics`
+
+`from_str`, `from_str_binary`, `from_str_octal`, `from_str_hex` on fully
+arbitrary text and on number-shaped strings (optional sign, long digit runs,
+decimal point, `e`/`E`/`@` exponents) must return `Err` rather than panic.
+Evidence: all parsers return `Result<_, ParseFixedError>`.
+
+### 4. The five parse variants agree
+`from_str::tests::hegel_parse_variants_agree`
+
+Taking `overflowing_from_str` as the source of truth: `from_str` succeeds iff
+no overflow, `wrapping_from_str` returns the wrapped value, and
+`saturating_from_str` clamps to `MAX`/`MIN` by the sign of the input string;
+all variants return the same `Err` for invalid input. Evidence: doc examples
+on `saturating_from_str`/`wrapping_from_str`/`overflowing_from_str` in
+`macros_from_to.rs`.
+
+### 5. Explicit-precision `Display` equals exact round-half-even decimal
+`display::tests::hegel_display_explicit_precision_matches_exact_rounding`
+
+`format!("{x:.p}")` for p in 0..=27 must equal an independent u128 oracle
+computing `bits / 2^frac` rounded to p decimal digits with ties to even
+(sign, integer part, and zero-padded fractional digits assembled manually).
+Evidence: crate-level docs, lib.rs: "The output is rounded to the nearest,
+with ties rounded to even." **This property found a real bug** (see
+Suspected bugs #2); known-buggy inputs excluded as in property 1.
+
+### 6/7. Addition and subtraction agree with a wide-integer oracle
+`arith::tests::hegel_add_agrees_with_wide_integer_oracle`,
+`arith::tests::hegel_sub_agrees_with_wide_integer_oracle`
+
+In bit representation the exact sum/difference is `a_bits ± b_bits` computed
+in i128. For 8 type aliases (signed/unsigned, 8–64 bits, frac 0..64):
+`checked_*` is `None` iff the exact result doesn't fit, `wrapping_*` is the
+truncation of the exact result, `overflowing_*` is `(wrapped, flag)`, and
+`saturating_*` clamps exactly to `MAX`/`MIN` by the sign of the exact result.
+
+### 8. Multiplication agrees with an exact floored wide oracle
+`arith::tests::hegel_mul_agrees_with_floored_wide_oracle`
+
+Exact product in bit representation is `(a_bits * b_bits) >> frac` (arithmetic
+shift = floor), computed in i128/u128. Same checked/wrapping/overflowing/
+saturating consistency as above, for 9 type aliases. Evidence: the
+double-width implementation in `arith.rs` (`mul_div_widen!`).
+
+### 9. Division agrees with an exact truncated wide oracle
+`arith::tests::hegel_div_agrees_with_truncated_wide_oracle`
+
+Exact quotient is `(a_bits << frac) / b_bits` truncated toward zero;
+`checked_div` is `None` for zero divisor or overflow; other variants
+consistent as above. The one case where even i128 overflows
+(`(i64::MIN << 64) / -1`) is asserted to report overflow.
+
+### 10. Negation overflows exactly at the boundary
+`arith::tests::hegel_neg_overflows_exactly_at_boundary`
+
+Signed: negation overflows iff `x == MIN`; unsigned: iff `x != 0`.
+`checked/wrapping/overflowing/saturating_neg` all agree with bit-level
+`wrapping_neg`, saturating to `MAX` (signed `MIN`) or `MIN` (unsigned).
+Covers 8-, 32- and 128-bit types.
+
+### 11. `abs` overflows exactly at `MIN`
+`arith::tests::hegel_abs_overflows_exactly_at_min`
+
+`checked/wrapping/overflowing/saturating_abs` agree with bit-level
+`wrapping_abs`; `unsigned_abs` never overflows and equals `bits.unsigned_abs()`
+in the unsigned twin type. Covers up to `I64F64`.
+
+### 12/13. Float conversions are exact round trips within precision
+`convert::tests::hegel_f64_roundtrip_exact_for_32bit_and_narrower`,
+`convert::tests::hegel_f32_roundtrip_exact_for_16bit_and_narrower`
+
+Every value of a fixed-point type of at most 32 bits is exactly representable
+in f64 (and 16 bits in f32), so `to_num::<f64>()` must equal
+`bits * 2^-frac` computed independently, and `from_num`/`checked_from_num`
+must return the original value bit-for-bit. Evidence: crate-level conversion
+docs (lossless when exactly representable).
+
+### 14. Lossless `From<integer>` conversions round trip
+`convert::tests::hegel_lossless_int_conversions_roundtrip`
+
+`From<iN/uN>` places the integer exactly in the integer bits
+(`(v as Bits) << frac`) and `to_num` recovers it, for 12 int/fixed pairs
+from i8/`I8F0` up to u128/`U128F0`. Evidence: "Infallible lossless
+conversions ... using `From`" in lib.rs.
+
+### 15. `sqrt` is rounded down with error < DELTA
+`sqrt::tests::hegel_sqrt_rounded_down_within_delta`
+
+Docs: "The result is rounded down, and the error is < DELTA. That is,
+result <= sqrt(self) < result + DELTA". With `x = X/2^f`, `r = R/2^f` this is
+exactly `R² <= X << f < (R+1)²`, checked in exact u128 arithmetic for 11
+type aliases (unsigned 8–64 bits, frac 0..64; signed restricted to the
+documented non-negative domain).
+
+### 16. All-fractional signed `sqrt` overflows exactly at 0.25
+`sqrt::tests::hegel_sqrt_all_frac_signed_overflows_at_quarter`
+
+Docs: for signed types with zero integer bits "overflow occurs for an input
+value >= 0.25". `I0F32::checked_sqrt` returns `None` iff `bits >= 1 << 30`,
+and satisfies the rounded-down contract below that.
+
+### Pinned known failures (deliberately failing tests)
+
+* `from_str::tests::hegel_known_failure_display_to_string_not_roundtrippable`
+* `display::tests::hegel_known_failure_display_explicit_precision_wrong_digits`
+
+Both draw from `sampled_from` over inputs that fail on every draw, so the
+failures are deterministic on every run. See Suspected bugs.
+
+## Test results
+
+* `cargo test` (default features): **132 passed, 2 failed** — the two
+  failures are exactly the deliberately pinned known-failure tests above.
+  All 16 property tests pass.
+* Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on every hegel test, then
+  reverted): no additional failures.
+* Liveness check: temporarily corrupting the add oracle (`+ 1`) produced an
+  immediate shrunk counterexample (`a_bits = 0, b_bits = 0`), then reverted.
+  The original (pre-exclusion) round-trip property also failed live with a
+  shrunk counterexample (`I0F8` bits 0x34), which became Suspected bug #1.
+* `cargo test --doc` run separately to confirm the doc tests still pass
+  (they are untouched by these changes).
+* No compiler warnings from the new tests.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Both bugs share a single root cause in `src/display.rs`,
+`FmtHelper::write_frac_dec` (the digit loop around line 533):
+
+```rust
+// Check if very close to zero, to avoid things like 0.19999999 and 0.20000001.
+if auto_prec && frac < Self::from(10) || Self::wrapping_neg(frac) < Self::from(10) {
+    buf.frac_digits = i + 1;
+    break;
+}
+```
+
+The loop stops emitting decimal digits as soon as the remaining fraction
+register comes within 10 register ULPs of 0 or 1.
+
+**Bug 1 — `Display` output does not identify the value (information loss).**
+`I16F16::from_bits(13108)` is exactly 0.20001220703125 but displays as
+`"0.2"`, which parses back (round-to-nearest) to `from_bits(13107)`;
+`from_bits(13107)` also displays as `"0.2"`, so the two values are
+indistinguishable in `Display` output. The `tie` logic in the same loop
+otherwise emits exactly enough digits for the output to parse back uniquely;
+the 10-ULP early break is strictly looser than the tie bound (10 vs 5 on the
+first digit) and much too coarse for narrow registers (10 ULPs of the u8
+register used by 8-bit types is 10/256 ≈ 4% of the range: `I0F8` bits 0x34 =
+0.203125 also displays as `"0.2"`). Pinned by
+`hegel_known_failure_display_to_string_not_roundtrippable` (fails on bits
+13108 and 6553 of `I16F16` on every run).
+
+**Bug 2 — explicit precision violates the documented rounding.** The
+crate-level docs promise "The output is rounded to the nearest, with ties
+rounded to even", but because `&&` binds tighter than `||` in the condition
+above, the `wrapping_neg(frac) < 10` half fires even when `auto_prec` is
+false, i.e. when the user asked for an explicit precision.
+`I16F16::from_bits(6553)` is exactly 0.0999908447265625, yet
+`format!("{:.16}", x)` prints `"0.1000000000000000"` (wrong by ~9e-6 with
+no rounding excuse: the exact value fits in 16 fractional digits). Pinned by
+`hegel_known_failure_display_explicit_precision_wrong_digits`.
+
+Even if the auto-precision behavior of Bug 1 were considered an intentional
+display heuristic, Bug 2 is a plain violation of the documented contract and
+looks like an operator-precedence mistake (`auto_prec && (a || b)` was
+presumably intended). No fix was applied, per instructions. Sibling
+properties 1 and 5 exclude the affected inputs via
+`display_frac_hits_lossy_break`, each with a comment pointing at the
+canonical pinned test.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+* **Exact `Display` round trip with no exclusions** — as written first, the
+  property failed on the bug above; rather than abandoning it, it was kept
+  with a precise exclusion of the known-buggy inputs plus the two pinned
+  failing tests. Nothing else was weakened.
+* **`{:e}` (LowerExp) -> `FromStr` round trip** — considered (the parser does
+  accept exponents) but not written: the exponent formatting path shares the
+  lossy early break, and modelling which inputs it corrupts through the
+  exponent-normalization path would have duplicated large parts of the
+  implementation as the exclusion condition. Deliberately left out rather
+  than asserting something unsound or blindly excluding too much.
+* **128-bit types in the decimal round-trip test with frac > 64**
+  (`U0F128`, `I0F128`) — the exclusion helper's register simulation would
+  need 256-bit arithmetic (`r * 10` overflows u128 for a u128 register), so
+  these two aliases are only covered by the radix round trip, no-panic, and
+  parse-variant properties. Frac counts up to 64 (including 128-bit types
+  `I64F64`/`U64F64`) are fully covered.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+* The skill's advice that full-bit-pattern generators plus round-trip
+  properties find real bugs was on point: the very first default-count run
+  of the first property found Bug 1; no 10x run was needed.
+* The "keep a canonical pinned failing test + exclude from siblings with a
+  comment" flow worked well, but making a pinned failure *deterministic* was
+  easiest with `sampled_from` over always-failing inputs; the skill's
+  suggestion ("boost the generator toward the counterexample's region and
+  raise test_cases") reads as probabilistic — an explicit mention of the
+  `sampled_from`-only pin pattern would help.
+* The no_std guidance in the Rust reference (`use std::{format,
+  string::ToString};` inside test modules, `use std::vec;` for `one_of!`)
+  was exactly what this crate needed; without it the `#[hegel::test]`
+  expansion errors (E0599 on `to_string`, "cannot find macro `format`") are
+  confusing.
+* There is no global case-count override, so the 10x exploratory pass was
+  done with `sed` over the attribute and reverted; a `HEGEL_TEST_CASES`-style
+  env var would make that less error-prone.
+* Drawing inside a per-type macro expansion (19 draws per test case) worked
+  fine and shrank well; the reference could mention that pattern for crates
+  whose "one type" is really a family of macro-generated types, since
+  writing a generic draw over `typenum`-parameterized types is not practical.
+
+[hegel]: https://hegel.dev
